cn.org.huansoft.ter-music (26.4.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Karaoke is now model based and always available. The old built-in
    centre-cancellation DSP and its alpha levels (0 / 0.60 / 1.00) are gone:
    vocal removal runs the bundled UVR-MDX-NET 3 ONNX model (29,704,436
    bytes, SHA-256
    e02220e80d8253f4c2209f8924298b2b686bbdf2868b788ff5500fb9bd94aadc) in
    a low-priority background thread (nice 10), never on the UI or playback
    thread. The three levels are the switch, so there is no
    feature toggle and no model picker: Off plays the original, Faint vocals
    plays 0.4 x the original mix plus 0.6 x the model accompaniment, and No
    vocals plays the model accompaniment only. One model run produces both
    stems. The accompaniment is written to a per-process session cache under
    /tmp/ter-music-karaoke-<uid>-<pid>/<model-id>/<track-hash>/ (mode 0700) as
    15-second segments - segNNN.inst.flac, segNNN.soft.flac and a
    segNNN.ready marker written last, indexed by manifest.txt. A complete
    legacy whole-track cache under $XDG_CACHE_HOME/ter-music/karaoke/ is
    still read, takes priority and remains covered by the 2048 MB LRU
    setting; session segments are not counted against it.

  * Segmented playback rules. At a level other than Off the first play of a
    track without a cache waits for the first accompaniment segment (about
    7-10 s on the test machine, hard cap 30 s, 8 s when playback starts at a
    non-zero position) and shows a preparation percentage; a segment that is
    late at a segment boundary or after a seek is waited for up to 8 s before
    the original takes over from that point. Changing the level while a track
    plays requests separation for the current track and switches the source
    at the current position once the target segment is ready (about 9-15 s
    for the first switch; Soft and Full switch immediately on ready
    segments); after a fallback the core re-acquires the accompaniment at
    most three times per level change and never reports a switch that did not
    happen. Seek-priority backfill, prefetch of the next queue entry with
    seamless_preload=1, and a CUE sub-track guard are included. Mono tracks,
    tracks longer than 30 minutes and CUE sub-tracks are not separated into
    session segments; any failure (missing or damaged model, unavailable
    runtime, timeout) keeps the original playing and names the reason on the
    status line. There is no lyric dependency and no older algorithm to fall
    back to.

  * The vocal-separation model now ships inside every package, so the
    feature needs no download and no configuration key: the build downloads
    and verifies the file, CMake checks size and whole-file SHA-256 again and
    installs it as <prefix>/share/ter-music/models/uvr-mdxnet-3-9662.onnx
    together with models/NOTICE.md (its MIT notice). Without the file a
    source build still starts, reports the model as missing and plays the
    original. <prefix>/share is authoritative; the old
    $XDG_DATA_HOME/ter-music/models/ location is read-only compatibility
    only.

  * Configuration contract 12 (breaking). Version 12 removes
    preferences.karaoke_feature and preferences.karaoke_model - the model is
    bundled, so the level alone selects the feature. Every config.xml below
    12 is migrated on the first start of the new core and rewritten with the
    numeric version="12"; every other key (karaoke_mode, theme, paths,
    equalizer, key bindings) keeps its value (older files also receive the
    one-shot defaults of the earlier migrations: background 0 to -1 below v4,
    core_exit_when_no_frontend=0 below v7, stop_core_on_exit=1 below v8,
    appearance_mode=auto below v10) and a second start leaves the file
    byte-identical. config get/set karaoke_feature now fails as an
    unknown key and Config.Set rejects a patch that still contains either
    removed key. Downgrades are not supported: a pre-v12 build reads the file
    (the version attribute parses as an integer and its migrations stay
    inactive), sees the removed keys as absent and writes the attribute back
    as 10 on its next save. Contract history: 4 was tag v2.1.0 (root attribute
    literal "2.2"), 7 introduced the resident core, 8 added
    stop_core_on_exit, 9 added the keybinding overrides table together with
    karaoke_mode, 10 added appearance_mode and 12 replaced the DSP karaoke
    with the bundled ONNX model. No contract-11 build was ever released, so
    no released file can contain the two removed elements; a hand-edited file
    that does loses them on the next save.

  * D-Bus api_version 7 (breaking) with the organization migration. The
    package, application id, source and header trees, Linyaps id, systemd
    unit and D-Bus interfaces all move to cn.org.huansoft.ter-music /
    cn.org.huansoft.ter_music.*, and the interface namespace is part of the
    wire protocol: a pre-7 front end calling the new core gets
    UnknownInterface, a new front end cannot reach an old core, and
    TER_MUSIC_API_MIN_VERSION rises from 4 to 7, so core and front ends must
    be upgraded as a pair. Unchanged on purpose: the MPRIS bus name
    org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.ter_music, the executable name, the data directory
    name and the config file location. The renamed package Replaces/Breaks
    the old ter-music package in place, so no manual removal is needed.

  * JSON layouts and a layout editor. The main area is a binary split tree of
    six panes (playlist, info, controls, spectrum, lyrics, vinyl), always
    force-tiled and resized by per-mille ratios, with seven presets (classic,
    vinyl, karaoke, visualizer, focus, stage, neon) in the shipped
    layouts.json. The layout editor edits the tree, saves user layouts to
    ~/.config/ter-music/layouts.json and materialises colours when a built-in
    preset is saved; TER_MUSIC_LAYOUTS overrides the file for development.
    Each pane can carry its own foreground and background colour in addition
    to the 24 theme palettes.

  * Terminal light/dark adaptation (contract 10). Settings -> Appearance ->
    Default Appearance Mode selects Auto (default), Light or Dark and is
    stored as preferences.appearance_mode. In Auto the TUI probes OSC 11
    before the first frame and then falls back to the deepin-terminal theme
    adapter, the XDG desktop portal color-scheme (followed live through
    SettingChanged), COLORFGBG, a TERM hint and finally the legacy dark look;
    the osc probe and the portal read are capped (about 200 ms and 120 ms)
    and only run in Auto, replies are stripped from the key stream, and
    factory colours and all seven presets switch to a light-safe palette
    while values changed by the user stay verbatim. The detected variant is
    per front-end process and is never written to config.xml; an optional
    per-terminal override lives in
    ~/.config/ter-music/appearance-terminals.json.

  * One file/folder chooser for every path entry point. Open folder, Append
    folder, language-pack install and the default music directory all use the
    same picker: zenity or kdialog (XDG portal as the preferred desktop
    route) when a graphical session is available, otherwise a typed path in a
    pure TTY without leaving the alternate screen.
    TERM_MUSIC_FILE_CHOOSER=portal|zenity|kdialog|tty forces a backend and an
    unknown value falls back to auto; empty input cancels and ~/ expands.

  * Mouse and menus. Left click selects and activates, the wheel scrolls and
    right click opens a context menu (middle click opens the same menu on
    terminals that intercept right click). On the Equalizer page a left click
    only selects a band and changes no gain while the wheel changes the
    selected band's gain by 1 dB (pre-amp the same, the enable row does not
    respond). Menu entrance and exit animations compose one frame per
    movement, degrade or switch off on slow terminals and in Aurora Flow,
    never delay input, and can be forced with
    TERM_MUSIC_MENU_ANIM=full|reduced|off.

  * Configurable key bindings (contract 9). Any action can be rebound in
    Settings -> Hotkeys and only modified bindings are written into the
    <keybindings> override table; ter-music keys list prints the action IDs
    and ter-music keys check validates the file, including cross-context
    shadowing and system-reserved keys (F1-F9, q/Q, Tab/Shift+Tab). Invalid
    entries are reported and ignored at run time instead of breaking the TUI.

  * Remote source hardening and UX. SMB, SFTP, FTP, WebDAV and HTTP all list
    in the front end and hand downloaded local paths to the core; per
    connection TLS (including ftps and webdavs), an explicit "trust
    self-signed certificate" switch, certificate verification by default and
    no HTTPS-to-HTTP downgrade. SFTP verifies known_hosts and fails closed
    with no silent trust-on-first-use; SMB uses the external smbclient with a
    mode-0600 auth file (the password never enters argv) and refuses file
    names that smbclient -c would split. Logs, last_opened_path and folder
    history store a redacted URL. The remote page shows download progress,
    supports cancel and retry of failed tracks, applies caps (1 GiB per file,
    32 MiB per listing, 200 tracks per open, 10/120/600 s timeouts) and keeps
    a 2048 MB LRU cache with protected playing/queued/favourited/history
    files.

  * Per-character lyrics and D-Bus API additions. LRC character,
    line-start/end and interpolated timings are used for per-character
    highlighting, and api_version 5 added the additive lyric timing fields
    (end, timing, char_total, chars, char_sung) plus the Lyrics.GetDocument
    paging used by the TUI. The three built-in language packs are zh_CN,
    en_US and the new zh-PQ (classical Chinese); CLI help, error and hint
    strings are localized as well, and --lang selects the language for this
    process (CLI or TUI) without persisting it.

  * Cross-distro packaging from one source tree. DEB built in Debian 10
    (glibc 2.28) covers Debian 10-13 and Ubuntu 20.04-26.04; four RPMs are
    built in EL7/8/9/10 containers because the ncursesw and libpng sonames
    differ, with libcurl < 7.32 and ncurses 5.x compatibility for EL7; one
    AppImage (glibc 2.28) and one AUR package (makepkg in an Arch container)
    complete the matrix. Every package installs real files under
    /opt/apps/cn.org.huansoft.ter-music/files and only six relative symlinks
    under /usr. FFmpeg is linked statically (audio-only build, no GPL
    components); libxml2 is not linked at all and util/xml_compat.c dlopens
    libxml2.so.2 or libxml2.so.16 at run time, so one binary runs on old and
    new distributions. Libcurl remains a hard build dependency and SMB needs
    the smbclient runtime program.

  * Build and release tooling cleanup. Packaging is now five packaging
    scripts (build-deb.sh, build-rpm.sh, build-appimage.sh, build-aur.sh,
    build-linyaps.sh) plus launch-auto-build.sh; the portable builder, the
    RPM-conversion path and the old six-builder set are gone, and the
    ONNX Runtime static-library helper (build-onnxruntime-static.sh) is a
    build dependency, not a package type. Every packaging script requires an
    explicit -v/--version: there is no detect_version fallback reading
    APP_VERSION or a git tag, and four-component versions such as 26.4.1.0
    are accepted instead of being truncated to 26.4.1. The
    source package, if requested with --with-source, carries the model so it
    rebuilds offline; DEB/RPM source and debuginfo packages stay optional.

  * Rolling release versioning. The version scheme is <yy>.<q>.<x>.<y> (year,
    quarter, line, fix), so this release is 26.4.1.0 and the Debian version
    is 26.4.1.0-1. It is written once in types.h APP_VERSION ("v26.4.1.0")
    and mirrored in the CMake project VERSION, the PKGBUILD pkgver, this
    changelog and the RPM spec default; packaging injects it with
    -DTER_MUSIC_VERSION and takes -v as the authority, and no step depends on
    a git tag.

  * TUI robustness and smaller fixes: the Linyaps ll-box <= 2.3.5 cold-start
    replay is bounded by measuring per-frame terminal writes and repainting
    only on burst, back-pressure or resize evidence (no timer repaint, and
    TERM_MUSIC_TTY_HARD_RESET=1 remains the opt-in endwin fallback); the
    cover scan now discards truncated path candidates instead of stat'ing
    them and picking up an unrelated image.

 -- HuanSoft Open Source Community - yxzl <yxzl666xx@outlook.com>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0800

ter-music (2.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop the playback core when a front end exits. New boolean config key
    preferences.stop_core_on_exit (default 1, also on the settings page as
    "Stop playback process on exit"): every remote/daemon front-end exit
    path (q, F9, Ctrl+C, SIGTERM, a lost TTY) converges on the shutdown
    routine and sends Control.Quit, so the core persists its session/resume
    state, stops playback and exits; with the option off (0) the front end
    sends Control.Detach instead and the core keeps playing in the
    background as in 2.3.0. Both messages are sent only when that front end
    really attached to the core (handshake completed, Attach token held); an
    exit from a front end that never attached sends not a single bus message
    and leaves somebody else's core running. The branch reads the front end's
    own configuration mirror, loaded at start-up and pushed to the core by
    the settings page, so the exit path no longer issues a synchronous read
    of the core's value. With several front ends, an exit from an attached
    front end stops the core unconditionally while the option is on; local
    mode is unchanged. Control.Quit is best effort and stays out of the
    required handshake methods. The configuration contract moves from version
    7 to 8:
    existing files are migrated once and receive the key at its default,
    which flips the default exit behaviour from "keep playing" to "stop the
    core" (breaking). The config.xml root version attribute is now written
    with the contract number ("8") instead of the old "2.2" literal;
    historical "2.2" files still load and are rewritten by the migration.
  * Never let two cores play at once after a front end exits. The core used to
    release its D-Bus name before it had stopped the audio, leaving a short
    window in which a front end started right after "q" saw no core, launched a
    second one and the two played the same music in parallel (and "stop" only
    silenced the instance that front end had attached to). The shutdown order
    is now: persist the session, stop and join the playback thread, destroy the
    backend stream, and only then release the bus name. The visible cost is
    that "daemon stop"/"restart" may see the instance go offline up to the
    playback-thread shutdown timeout (2s) plus the stream teardown later than
    before, which the CLI's 5s/10s wait budgets still cover.
  * Never let a remote front end play audio in its own process. With
    resume_last_playback enabled, the start-up resume path used to run before
    player_init(), so player_backend() still returned the facade's default
    LOCAL and the TUI created a real playback thread and PipeWire stream for a
    track the core was also playing, producing doubled audio (most audible
    with PipeWire, whose on-demand backend fallback made the accidental local
    stream succeed). After the front-end/backend split the resume path only
    records the selection and plays through the facade once the core is
    connected and the queue is delivered; as a second line of defence
    play_audio() refuses to run unless init_audio_device() ran in this
    process, and UI cleanup tears down any local audio engine (stop, join,
    backend stream) before the front end exits. A front end therefore only
    ever owns content and D-Bus commands, never an audio thread.
  * Report and persist the audible position, not the prebuffered write
    position. The playback thread counted every sample it had written into
    PipeWire's 1 MB ring (~2.9 s at 44.1 kHz stereo) as progress, while the
    process callback consumes that ring at real time. The UI, MPRIS and
    session resume therefore ran seconds ahead of what the user heard; after
    q the saved position included the unplayed ring and a restart appeared to
    have advanced during the pause. audio_get_position_seconds() now subtracts
    the backend's unplayed frames, and persist_playback_session_state() saves
    that corrected position. PipeWire reports the ring occupancy; Pulse/ALSA,
    whose writes are already bounded by the device, report zero.
  * Call PipeWire stream controls from the thread loop's locking context.
    pw_stream_set_active/set_control/flush were invoked without
    pw_thread_loop_lock(), so libpipewire diagnosed every pause, resume, flush
    and volume change as "called from wrong context" and the log filled with
    those lines. The four controls now take the loop lock (after dropping the
    internal state mutex, to keep the lock order deadlock-free). The audible
    volume/pause behaviour is unchanged; the diagnostics are gone.

 -- Yanxi Bamboo Forest <yxzl666xx@outlook.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:55:42 +0800

ter-music (2.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Split the player into a resident core plus front ends. The core owns the
    audio stack and the D-Bus service; the TUI and every CLI subcommand are
    clients that hand it content and commands. Closing the TUI (q, F9 or
    Ctrl+C) no longer interrupts playback, and a front end starts a core when
    none runs (inside a Linyaps container it asks the bus to activate instead
    of forking a detached process).
  * D-Bus API is now api_version 4, published as org.yxzl.ter_music.Info,
    Control, Lyrics, Queue and Config. The content interfaces (Playlist,
    Library, Favorites, History, DirHistory) and Remote are gone: the SQLite
    library, playlists, favorites, folder history and remote downloads are
    front-end data. The D-Bus scan job, Control.OpenPath/PlayIndex and
    Queue.Rebuild were removed, and MPRIS OpenUri answers NotSupported.
  * The play queue is a list of local paths delivered by the front end in
    chunks of at most 500 entries (one Queue.Set, then Queue.Append for the
    rest); an oversized single call is rejected as a whole with
    Error.InvalidArgs. Queue.Get is paged and part of the introspection.
    Nothing is persisted to queue.txt any more, and the resume session (last
    track plus position) is written by the core on every track change.
  * MPRIS parity: Rate is a real read/write playback rate (0.5-3.0), and
    LoopStatus and Shuffle switch whole play modes instead of storing two
    flags. The front-end registry (Control.Attach/Ping/Detach/FrontendInfo,
    2 s heartbeat, 6 s expiry) tracks live clients; an optional
    core_exit_when_no_frontend watchdog (config.xml, off by default) stops the
    core 10 s after the last front end leaves.
  * Command-line mode: play, pause, resume, toggle, stop, next, prev, seek,
    volume, speed, mode, show and daemon start|foreground|stop|restart|status|
    reload, plus the new queue list|append|insert|remove|clear|shuffle,
    config get|set|reload|reset, lyrics, track and cover subcommands. play
    accepts "~" and relative paths, hits an exact audio file, takes --index,
    --mode and --foreground, and without arguments keeps playing the current
    queue. Exit codes: 0 success, 1 usage error, 3 no running instance,
    4 D-Bus unavailable, 5 refused or operation failed.
  * Settings: new "Reload Config" and "Restore Defaults" actions, an equalizer
    preset dialog and an output-latency dialog as standalone popups, and an
    info display page (preset, fields, text cover size and charset, progress
    style, lyric lines) that drives `ter-music show` and the D-Bus output.
    The configuration contract is version 7 (config.xml keeps its 2.2 XML
    version attribute), migrated automatically from v1 JSON and from older
    contract versions.
  * Fix a startup SIGSEGV on directory trees deeper than ~7 levels caused by
    multi-megabyte stack frames in the recursive scan and playlist loading;
    the CUE sheet and the other large frames now live on the heap.
  * Resolve config, data and cache directories through XDG so Linyaps packages
    keep their data on the host. The systemd user unit and the D-Bus activation
    file for org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.ter_music are installed only by the Linyaps
    build (cmake -DINSTALL_LINYAPS_INTEGRATION=ON, which is OFF by default);
    this Debian package ships neither.

